A stiff shoulder — capsulitis and other explanations (@shoulder-stiffness: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @shoulder, #source @med-source-frozen, #source @med-source-msd-shoulder, #medicine @shoulder-instability, #orthopedics @shoulder-instability, #shoulder @shoulder-instability) { r_4JWG5NRDXS1ZHGKN05W8J3TAH2
**Read a small, coherent clinical account:** [Frozen shoulder — AAOS](../../../sources/Frozen-shoulder-AAOS.lingua#r_0S11NBE3K6SNW505KKMQC4M12Q), the description of capsular change, symptoms, and clinical assessment, then the range-of-motion discussion in [Avaliação do ombro — Manual MSD](../../../sources/Avaliacao-do-ombro-MSD.lingua#r_51H0EB6JRBDW2X2HNEBY52FG6X). Pay particular attention to active versus passive movement; preserve the wider differential diagnosis instead of treating one restricted direction as conclusive.

Adhesive capsulitis/capsulite adesiva or ombro congelado involves pain and restricted shoulder movement associated with capsular changes. It is not simply a loose phrase for every shoulder that is difficult to move.

Restrictions of active and passive movement, their pattern, clinical history, and other findings help distinguish possible explanations. Joint arthritis, injury, pain-limited movement, and postoperative restrictions can create different stiffness questions. Diabetes and some other conditions are relevant associations rather than proof of the diagnosis.

Time course varies. A sequence of named phases can organize a description but should not be treated as an exact recovery calendar. Treatment discussions weigh the person's stage, symptoms, function, and uncertainty.

**Rotation is informative only in context:** clinicians assess the pattern of restriction and compare active and passive motion with the rest of the findings. Progressive restriction, pain near one position, and a feeling of instability are different histories. A loss of external rotation can be part of a stiffness assessment; it is not a stand-alone diagnosis or a reason to force the joint to match a published angle.

**Try:** compare two fictional descriptions: limited active movement with much better passive movement, and restriction of both. Explain what each adds to reasoning and why neither is enough to prescribe a stretching program or establish a diagnosis. Keep movement observations distinct from forceful attempts to increase range.

**Continue:** [Instability, subluxation, and dislocation](Instability.lingua#r_4ANS5B79S566BS78A7E9H5X8R6).

